        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size humidifier do I need for a large room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Size by output, not floor area. A large closed room in a tight house typically needs 2 to 3 gallons a day, while an open living area in an average house can need 4 to 6 or more. Tall ceilings and drafty construction push that higher. Treat box coverage figures as a best case.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can one humidifier cover a whole open-plan floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if it has enough output. Large evaporative consoles like the Levoit Superior 6000S and AIRCARE MA1201 are rated for several thousand square feet and several gallons a day. Expect less in a drafty house, and run your furnace fan on circulate to help spread the moisture around.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is evaporative or ultrasonic better for a big room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Evaporative is usually better. Evaporative units scale to high output, leave no white dust, and are hard to over-run because output falls as humidity rises. Ultrasonics are quieter but need low-mineral water at large-room output, and can leave damp spots near the unit if run hard.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to run a large-room humidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Evaporative units are cheap to run, at roughly 10 to 50 watts listed for the picks here, which is pennies a day. Warm-mist ultrasonics draw far more, with the Levoit LV600HH rated at 280 watts. Wicks or distilled water often cost more over a winter than the electricity does.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity should a large room be in winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aim for 30 to 50 percent, measured by a hygrometer away from the humidifier. On very cold nights, drop toward 30 to 35 percent, because higher humidity condenses on windows and cold exterior walls. Fogged or dripping windows are the signal to turn the target down.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why can't my humidifier raise the humidity in my living room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually because it is undersized for the real volume of air or the rate at which cold outdoor air leaks in. Open stairwells, vaulted ceilings and drafty windows all multiply the load. Check its output in gallons per day against the space, and seal obvious drafts before buying a bigger unit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I get a whole-house humidifier instead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you need consistent humidity across more than one floor or more than about 2,000 square feet, a furnace-mounted whole-house humidifier is often the better answer. It never needs refilling and reaches every room through the ducts. It needs forced-air heat, a water line and usually professional installation.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

